1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,121
Welcome to this captioned walkthrough of Wayline, a weather aware truck
route planner built with React and Django.

2
00:00:07,121 --> 00:00:15,666
The goal is to help a driver compare road routes using the weather they are
expected to encounter, their cargo weight, and

3
00:00:15,666 --> 00:00:16,995
their travel time.

4
00:00:16,995 --> 00:00:20,603
This walkthrough uses the deployed application and actual forecasts.

5
00:00:20,603 --> 00:00:27,819
We will enter a trip, compare alternatives, inspect arrival time
checkpoints, explore the forty eight hour corridor layer,

6
00:00:27,819 --> 00:00:29,908
and briefly review the implementation.

7
00:00:29,908 --> 00:00:35,414
The app does not use a language model to decide which route to recommend.

8
00:00:35,414 --> 00:00:38,642
Its decisions come from the assignment's explicit rules.

9
00:00:38,642 --> 00:00:41,708
The form starts with an origin and destination.

10
00:00:41,708 --> 00:00:48,650
Place search returns labeled candidates, and a candidate must be selected
so the backend receives coordinates rather than an

11
00:00:48,650 --> 00:00:49,912
ambiguous city name.

12
00:00:49,912 --> 00:00:52,977
Here the demonstration trip is Chicago to Detroit.

13
00:00:52,977 --> 00:01:00,370
Departure is shown in the browser's local timezone and is sent to the API
as a timestamp with a timezone.

14
00:01:00,370 --> 00:01:02,714
Cargo weight is entered in pounds.

15
00:01:02,714 --> 00:01:05,419
It is separate from gross vehicle weight.

16
00:01:05,419 --> 00:01:09,746
The checkpoint buttons select ten, twenty five, or fifty mile spacing.

17
00:01:09,746 --> 00:01:12,812
A shorter interval samples the journey more closely.

18
00:01:12,812 --> 00:01:19,123
The origin and destination are included even when the final segment is
shorter than the selected interval.

19
00:01:19,123 --> 00:01:23,360
For this trip, I return the spacing to twenty five miles.

20
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:31,191
Finding routes calls the Django backend, which requests road geometry and
travel times, samples checkpoints along the road,

21
00:01:31,191 --> 00:01:33,071
and obtains hourly forecasts.

22
00:01:33,071 --> 00:01:36,621
Valhalla supplies truck routes without requiring a key.

23
00:01:36,621 --> 00:01:40,589
The app requests a primary route and two alternatives.

24
00:01:40,589 --> 00:01:49,256
A provider can return fewer distinct routes, and the app displays those
real results rather than inventing a third option.

25
00:01:49,256 --> 00:01:57,296
Each card shows road mileage, estimated driving duration, arrival time,
severe miles, high risk miles, and the distance

26
00:01:57,296 --> 00:01:58,758
weighted average risk.

27
00:01:58,758 --> 00:02:03,561
Clicking another card highlights its route and displays that route's
checkpoints.

28
00:02:03,561 --> 00:02:09,722
Selection is separate from the recommendation, so every returned option
remains available for comparison.

29
00:02:09,722 --> 00:02:13,435
A checkpoint popup connects the forecast to the journey.

30
00:02:13,435 --> 00:02:21,643
It shows the route, cumulative mileage, estimated arrival, sustained wind
in miles per hour, rain and snowfall in inches per

31
00:02:21,643 --> 00:02:25,063
hour, and the final risk with its reason.

32
00:02:25,063 --> 00:02:32,978
These are forecasts matched to the checkpoint's arrival time, rather than
the same weather hour being reused for the entire

33
00:02:32,978 --> 00:02:33,564
trip.

34
00:02:33,564 --> 00:02:36,105
Checkpoints follow the actual road polyline.

35
00:02:36,105 --> 00:02:42,652
Travel time comes from provider edge timing or maneuver timing, with
distance based interpolation where needed.

36
00:02:42,652 --> 00:02:45,974
The destination is included as the last checkpoint.

37
00:02:45,974 --> 00:02:50,860
The displayed forecast hour ends the precipitation interval containing the
arrival time.

38
00:02:50,860 --> 00:02:56,332
These travel estimates exclude driver breaks, fuel stops, and hours of
service scheduling.

39
00:02:56,332 --> 00:02:59,749
The weather outlook is a different view of time.

40
00:02:59,749 --> 00:03:05,325
It shows a two dimensional grid around the returned routes at one common
forecast hour.

41
00:03:05,325 --> 00:03:11,530
The controls switch between physical wind, rain, and snow values, with
units and a color scale.

42
00:03:11,530 --> 00:03:17,106
Moving the slider selects any hour from the displayed base through plus
forty eight hours.

43
00:03:17,106 --> 00:03:22,772
The grid series is fetched separately from route planning, so slider
changes are handled locally.

44
00:03:22,772 --> 00:03:26,908
They do not recalculate checkpoint risks or change the route
recommendation.

45
00:03:26,908 --> 00:03:29,247
Those remain tied to arrival times.

46
00:03:29,247 --> 00:03:33,024
The layer can also be hidden to inspect the roads.

47
00:03:33,024 --> 00:03:36,171
Gray cells mean weather is unavailable, not zero.

48
00:03:36,171 --> 00:03:40,758
Cell count is capped, and longer corridors use a coarser displayed
resolution.

49
00:03:40,758 --> 00:03:47,966
The risk module applies the assignment's wind, rain, snow, and load
thresholds without rounding the input values first.

50
00:03:47,966 --> 00:03:53,769
Wind at or above fifty five miles per hour means no travel for any load.

51
00:03:53,769 --> 00:04:01,258
Wind from forty five up to fifty five, with cargo strictly above thirty
thousand pounds, also means no travel.

52
00:04:01,258 --> 00:04:09,121
Wind from thirty five up to forty five, with cargo strictly above forty
thousand pounds, raises the level to severe.

53
00:04:09,121 --> 00:04:12,303
Exact weight boundaries do not trigger those overrides.

54
00:04:12,303 --> 00:04:17,358
The final checkpoint risk is the highest applicable weather level or load
override.

55
00:04:17,358 --> 00:04:22,038
A lower wind risk never cancels a higher rain or snow risk.

56
00:04:22,038 --> 00:04:28,590
This particular live trip has benign conditions; the hazardous boundary
cases are exercised with labeled automated fixtures.

57
00:04:28,590 --> 00:04:32,008
Open Meteo supplies sustained wind at ten meters.

58
00:04:32,008 --> 00:04:34,621
Wind is interpolated between hourly timestamps.

59
00:04:34,621 --> 00:04:42,259
Rain uses rain plus showers, converted from millimeters to inches, while
snowfall is converted from centimeters to inches.

60
00:04:42,259 --> 00:04:49,797
Accumulations refer to the preceding hour, so the code selects the interval
ending at or after the arrival.

61
00:04:49,797 --> 00:04:54,018
It never silently clamps an arrival outside the forecast window.

62
00:04:54,018 --> 00:05:01,556
A segment takes the worse risk of its endpoints, and actual segment mileage
includes the last partial segment.

63
00:05:01,556 --> 00:05:04,169
Average risk is weighted by distance.

64
00:05:04,169 --> 00:05:11,506
Eligible routes are compared first by severe miles, then high risk miles,
average risk, and travel time.

65
00:05:11,506 --> 00:05:16,934
Any no travel segment or unknown required weather excludes a route from
recommendation.

66
00:05:16,934 --> 00:05:21,229
The frontend uses React, TypeScript, Vite, and Leaflet.

67
00:05:21,229 --> 00:05:28,312
Django exposes small JSON endpoints for place search, trip planning,
corridor forecasts, and health.

68
00:05:28,312 --> 00:05:35,394
Provider access, geometry, forecast matching, and pure risk functions are
separated in the backend.

69
00:05:35,394 --> 00:05:43,754
Requests have timeouts, weather locations are fetched in bounded batches,
and short lived caches reduce repeat calls.

70
00:05:43,754 --> 00:05:46,308
Stale frontend requests are canceled.

71
00:05:46,308 --> 00:05:52,462
No database, user accounts, background worker, or runtime AI service is
required.

72
00:05:52,462 --> 00:06:01,634
The frontend and backend are hosted as separate Vercel projects, with the
frontend origin explicitly allowed by the API.

73
00:06:01,634 --> 00:06:09,297
Thirteen backend tests pass, including exact thresholds, strict load
boundaries, weighted ranking, missing forecasts, timing,

74
00:06:09,297 --> 00:06:11,387
and cross origin access.

75
00:06:11,387 --> 00:06:16,728
The production build and a live deployed walkthrough were also checked.

76
00:06:16,728 --> 00:06:20,321
The README records the approximations and remaining limitations.

77
00:06:20,321 --> 00:06:27,085
Public routing and weather services can throttle requests, return fewer
alternatives, or provide incomplete forecasts.

78
00:06:27,085 --> 00:06:35,434
If truck routing is unavailable, the standard road fallback is clearly
labeled because it does not check truck restrictions.

79
00:06:35,434 --> 00:06:42,832
The truck profile otherwise uses provider defaults; cargo is not
incorrectly passed as the vehicle's gross weight.

80
00:06:42,832 --> 00:06:47,693
Weather between samples and travel time within a maneuver are estimates.

81
00:06:47,693 --> 00:06:55,725
The app follows the assignment's risk thresholds and is a planning
demonstration rather than a guarantee of safe road

82
00:06:55,725 --> 00:06:56,359
conditions.

83
00:06:56,359 --> 00:07:02,700
The repository includes setup instructions, tests, provider attribution,
deployment configuration, and the verified live

84
00:07:02,700 --> 00:07:03,334
URLs.

85
00:07:03,334 --> 00:07:10,520
This edited video uses captured views of the actual app, source excerpts,
and actual test output.

86
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:12,422
Thank you for watching.
